        <p class="profile-detail-region">Battle · Central &amp; East Asia</p>
        <h1 class="profile-detail-name">Battle of Nagashino</h1>
        <div class="info-stats" style="margin-top:10px;">
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Date</span><span class="info-stat-val">June 1575: the siege of Nagashino Castle began that month, the armies faced each other on 28 June and the main battle was fought on 29 June, from early morning until about 2 pm according to the Shinchō Kōki</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Place</span><span class="info-stat-val">Nagashino Castle and Shitaragahara, about 4 km to its west, in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mikawa-province">Mikawa Province</a> (present-day Shinshiro, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aichi-prefecture">Aichi Prefecture</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>)</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">The two sides</span><span class="info-stat-val"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oda-nobunaga">Oda Nobunaga</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-ieyasu">Tokugawa Ieyasu</a>, with 38,000 men by the usual figure (about 30,000 Oda and about 8,000 Tokugawa troops); Takeda Katsuyori, with 15,000, of whom 12,000 marched to the main battle and 3,000 stayed at the siege</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Outcome</span><span class="info-stat-val">The Oda-Tokugawa forces won, and the Takeda army lost many of its commanders; the Shinchō Kōki puts Takeda losses at 10,000 men. Katsuyori withdrew to Kai Province.</span></div>
        </div>
