    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>The Takeda army of about 15,000 faced an Oda-Tokugawa force put at 38,000, and recent research stresses the allied superiority in troops and firepower over any new tactic.</li><li>The familiar story of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oda-nobunaga">Nobunaga</a>'s three volleys fired in turn by 3,000 guns comes from later war tales and a 1903 military history; the Shinchō Kōki mentions about 1,000 guns and no volleys in turn.</li><li>Many Takeda commanders died, but according to recent research most of them were killed during the retreat, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/takeda-clan">Takeda clan</a> lasted until 1582.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>Torii Suneemon, sent out of besieged Nagashino Castle to ask <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-ieyasu">Ieyasu</a> for reinforcements, was captured on his way back; offered his life if he called out that no help was coming, he shouted that help would certainly come, and he was crucified.</p></aside>
    </section>

