    <section class="rise-fall" id="timeline" aria-labelledby="timeline-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">1573</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/takeda-shingen">Takeda Shingen</a> falls ill during a campaign in Mikawa and the Takeda forces withdraw to Kai Province while concealing his death</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1575</span><span class="rf-text">Tokugawa forces under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/honda-tadakatsu">Honda Tadakatsu</a> and Sakakibara Yasumasa take Nagashino Castle, and Takeda Katsuyori leads his main force into Okumikawa</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">June 1575</span><span class="rf-text">Katsuyori besieges Nagashino Castle, held by 500 men, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-ieyasu">Ieyasu</a> asks <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oda-nobunaga">Oda Nobunaga</a> for help</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">June 1575</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oda-nobunaga">Nobunaga</a> leaves <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gifu">Gifu</a> with about 30,000 men and joins <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-ieyasu">Ieyasu</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/okazaki">Okazaki</a> the next day</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">June 1575</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oda-nobunaga">Nobunaga</a> halts at Shitaragahara and the allies build fences and earthworks</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">June 1575</span><span class="rf-text">Katsuyori marches east of Shitaragahara with 12,000 men and the two armies face each other across the Rengo River</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">June 1575</span><span class="rf-text">Sakai Tadatsugu's detachment attacks from behind Mount Tobigasu, and the Takeda army attacks the allied lines and is routed</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1582</span><span class="rf-text">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/takeda-clan">Takeda clan</a> is destroyed, seven years after Nagashino</span></li></ol>
    </section>

    <section class="battle-field" id="how-it-was-fought" data-battle="battle-of-nagashino" data-v="b8dc666e6d" aria-labelledby="battle-field-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="battle-field-title">How it was fought</h2>
      <p class="bf-lede">The field and the armies, phase by phase. Press Play, or step through with Next and Back.</p>
      <ol class="bf-phases"><li><b>The allies camp at Shitaragahara</b> Nobunaga halted at Shitaragahara without going to the relief of Nagashino Castle and camped west of it, Nobutada on Mount Shinmido and Ieyasu on Mount Takamatsu facing the river. The Takeda were still around the castle.</li><li><b>Takeda marches east of Shitaragahara</b> Katsuyori led his main force towards Shitaragahara and left 3,000 men at the siege. The Oda and Tokugawa forces advanced, and the two sides faced each other across the Rengo River.</li><li><b>Sakai sets out by night</b> Nobunaga took a suggestion from Sakai Tadatsugu and ordered a surprise attack on Mount Tobigasu. Sakai set out in the middle of the night with a flying column of about 4,000 men.</li><li><b>Sakai attacks the fort from behind</b> Sakai's column fell on the Takeda soldiers defending the fort from behind Mount Tobigasu. The soldiers in the fort were defeated, and the enemy now stood behind Katsuyori's main army.</li><li><b>The column joins the castle and drives off the besiegers</b> The detachment entered the castle in cooperation with its own soldiers and drove the Takeda forces off. Katsuyori's main army was left with its retreat cut off and the enemy behind it.</li><li><b>The Takeda charge the allied camps</b> With nowhere to go, the Takeda main force attacked the Tokugawa and Oda camps in front of it, one after the other.</li><li><b>Nobunaga takes command and sends up the matchlocks</b> Nobunaga came to Ieyasu's camp on Mount Takamatsu to see how the fighting stood and took command of the whole army there. He then sent a matchlock corps led by five bugyō to the front line.</li><li><b>Guns and fences stop the charges</b> The Takeda charged repeatedly, and each time the matchlock guns and the horse defence fences blocked them and they fell back.</li><li><b>The Takeda forces begin their rout</b> By midday it was increasingly clear which side had won, and the Takeda forces began their rout, their main force falling back from the allied camps towards the east.</li><li><b>Nobunaga falls on the broken ranks</b> With many of their troops killed, the Takeda army retreated. Nobunaga pounced on them when their ranks gave way and inflicted even more casualties; the fighting lasted until around 2 pm.</li></ol>
    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="background">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Background</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/oda-nobunaga"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/oda-nobunaga.jpg" alt="Oda Nobunaga" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/oda-nobunaga">Oda Nobunaga</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Odanobunaga.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">狩野宗秀 (Kanō Sōshū, 1551 - 1601), Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The trigger was the contest between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-ieyasu">Tokugawa Ieyasu</a> and Takeda Katsuyori over North Mikawa. The Takeda and Tokugawa clans had been at war since the lifetime of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/takeda-shingen">Takeda Shingen</a>. The Takeda forces were overwhelmingly superior in strength, and the Okudaira clan and other provincial leaders of Okumikawa in the north-east of Mikawa joined the Takeda side to prolong their lives. Ieyasu had a powerful ally in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oda-nobunaga">Oda Nobunaga</a>, who held the capital and was rapidly increasing his power, but Nobunaga was by no means an equal ally, and used Ieyasu as a bulwark against the Takeda.</p><p>When Shingen fell ill during a campaign in Mikawa in May 1573, the Takeda forces retreated to Kai Province and worked to transfer power to Katsuyori while concealing Shingen's death. Ieyasu, sensing something unusual in their movements, set out to reverse the situation and manoeuvred the kunishū, the powerful families of Okumikawa, to regain the north of Mikawa. His forces sent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/honda-tadakatsu">Honda Tadakatsu</a> and Sakakibara Yasumasa to take Nagashino Castle, which gave Ieyasu a strategic transport point and a base in the region.</p><p>Katsuyori, having finished his succession, led the main force of the Takeda army into Okumikawa. He moved south along the Toyokawa River and ravaged the whole of East Mikawa, launching raids throughout the estates of the Tokugawa <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassals</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="forces">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">The Two Sides</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The allied forces of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oda-nobunaga">Nobunaga</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-ieyasu">Ieyasu</a> are put at 38,000 and Katsuyori's at 15,000. Katsuyori besieged Nagashino Castle, which had only 500 defenders, with 15,000 men. Ieyasu could mobilise only about 8,000 troops, and Nobunaga came with an Oda force of about 30,000 and his heir, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oda-nobutada">Oda Nobutada</a>. On 28 June Katsuyori marched to the east of Shitaragahara with a main force of 12,000 and left 3,000 men at the siege.</p><p>Nobunaga made his main camp on Mount Gokurakuji, west of Shitaragahara, and Nobutada camped on Mount Shinmido. Ieyasu camped on Mount <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/takamatsu">Takamatsu</a>, facing the Rengo River. Shitaragahara is a depression, and Nobunaga placed his 30,000 men so that they did not look like a large force from the Takeda side. The Shinchō Kōki says the allies steadily built positions with horse defence fences and earthworks.</p><p>The Shinchō Kōki records that Nobunaga deployed about 1,000 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/matchlock">matchlock</a> guns between five commanders, Sassa Narimasa, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maeda-toshiie">Maeda Toshiie</a>, Nonomura Masanari, Fukuzumi Hidekatsu and Ban Naomasa. Some <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscripts</a> read 3,000, and it is unclear who changed the number. A further 500 guns went with Sakai Tadatsugu's detachment. Takeda military registers show that the Takeda army also held about 1,000 to 1,500 guns. Neither army had units made up wholly of horsemen: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassals</a> brought cavalry, spearmen and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/archery">archers</a> according to their lands and wealth, and these fought in groups.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="course">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">How It Went</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>In June 1575 Katsuyori invaded Mikawa and surrounded Nagashino Castle. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-ieyasu">Ieyasu</a> asked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oda-nobunaga">Nobunaga</a> for help, and Nobunaga left <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gifu">Gifu</a> on 21 June and joined Ieyasu at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/okazaki">Okazaki</a> on 22 June. Torii Suneemon was sent out of the castle to Okazaki to ask for reinforcements. On his way back with the news that help was coming he was captured, and was promised his life and a reward if he shouted to the castle that no help would come. He shouted that reinforcements would certainly come and was crucified.</p><p>Nobunaga reached Shitaragahara on 26 June and halted instead of relieving the castle. Katsuyori's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassals</a> advised him to retreat to Kai, but he judged the enemy weak, since the allies did not come to the castle's relief and were fortifying, and he decided to fight. After the armies faced each other across the Rengo River, Nobunaga took up a suggestion from the Tokugawa vassal Sakai Tadatsugu. Sakai set out at night with a column of about 4,000 men and early on 29 June attacked the Takeda troops from behind Mount Tobigasu. The detachment joined the men of Nagashino Castle and drove the Takeda forces off.</p><p>With its retreat cut off, the main Takeda army charged the Oda and Tokugawa camps in front of it one after another. Nobunaga came to Ieyasu's camp on Mount <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/takamatsu">Takamatsu</a> and took command, sending a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/matchlock">matchlock</a> corps under five bugyō to the front. Repeated Takeda charges were stopped by the guns and fences, and by midday the Takeda forces were routed. The Shinchō Kōki says the battle lasted until about 2 pm, and that Nobunaga pressed the retreating Takeda and inflicted further casualties.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="aftermath">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Aftermath</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/takeda-shingen"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/takeda-shingen.jpg" alt="Takeda Shingen" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/takeda-shingen">Takeda Shingen</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Takeda_Harunobu.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>According to the Shinchō Kōki the Takeda lost 10,000 men, two-thirds of the force that had besieged the castle. Several of the Twenty-Four Generals of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/takeda-shingen">Takeda Shingen</a> died, among them Baba Nobuharu, Hara Masatane, Sanada Nobutsuna and his younger brother Sanada Masateru, Yamagata Masakage, Saegusa Moritomo, Tsuchiya Masatsugu, Naitō Masatoyo and Obata Masamori. Yamagata Masakage was killed by a gunshot. Recent research holds that most of the Takeda commanders died during the retreat and not in the fighting.</p><p>Katsuyori could no longer afford to conquer other territories and withdrew to his stronghold in Kai. In early July, immediately after the victory, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-ieyasu">Ieyasu</a> led his army to retake one castle after another that the Takeda had captured. Katsuyori remained a stubborn opponent and went on troubling Ieyasu by regrouping as he retreated. In 1577 a Takeda general, Miura Uemonnosuke, tried to take Hōkizuka Castle and failed against the Tokugawa general Abe Motozane.</p><p>Katsuyori instructed his troops to prepare 200 to 300 rounds of ammunition per gun after the battle. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/takeda-clan">Takeda clan</a> was not destroyed until 1582, seven years after the defeat at Nagashino and Shitaragahara.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="sources">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">How We Know</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Shinchō Kōki is the chief account and is regarded as highly reliable. It gives the strength of the gun corps as 1,000, the five commanders, the length of the battle and the Takeda losses, and it does not mention volleys fired in turn. The Mikawa Monogatari, written by Ōkubo Tadataka, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-ieyasu">Ieyasu</a>, does not even give the number of guns. The tale of three-stage shooting comes from the Shinchōki, a war tale written by the Edo-period Confucian scholar Oze Hoan, and was carried into Nihon Senshi Nagashino no Eki, a history compiled by the Imperial Japanese Army General Staff Office in 1903. Tokutomi Sohō's Kinsei Nihon Kokuminshi (1918 to 1952) followed that account entirely.</p><p>Doubts about the volleys were first voiced by the amateur researcher Fujimoto Masayuki in his 1980 essay Nagashino no Teppō Senjutsu wa Kyokō da, and the prevailing view now is that three-stage shooting by 3,000 guns was a later invention. Research in the 2010s added new findings and interpretations. Scientific analysis of shot found on the battlefield shows that the lead in the allied shot was of three types, domestic, South Chinese and Korean, and Thai, while the Takeda shot is nearly identical in composition to imported Chinese coins, suggesting that it was made by melting <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a> coins.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The battle is often cited as a turning point in Japanese warfare and as the first modern battle in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>, and after it <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oda-nobunaga">Nobunaga</a>'s unification of the country was seen as certain. It came to be remembered as the battle in which Katsuyori recklessly drove his cavalry against long horse defence fences where Nobunaga waited with 3,000 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/matchlock">matchlock</a> guns. A myth grew that Nobunaga was a military genius who devised three-stage shooting, and the scene has been depicted repeatedly in films, television dramas and other fiction. The historical community at the time of Sohō held a similar view, which lasted long after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a>.</p><p>Recent research has overturned this belief, and Katsuyori is being re-evaluated. The allied victory is attributed to their superiority in firepower and troop numbers, Nobunaga's placing of his forces, and the Takeda misjudging their opponents' strength and attacking head on. Charging at the enemy line was common in battles of the time, and the term Takeda cavalry is inappropriate. The Takeda had promoted guns since Shingen's time but found lead, nitrates and guns hard to obtain, while Nobunaga controlled the port of Sakai and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyoto">Kyoto</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kinai">Kinai</a> trade and bought materials from overseas. Recent writing calls the battle one between the Oda, with abundant supplies in the west, and the Takeda, who lacked them in the east. The battle is now often named the Battle of Nagashino and Shitaragahara, after the castle and the place where the final fighting took place.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>In June 1575 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oda-nobunaga">Oda Nobunaga</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-ieyasu">Tokugawa Ieyasu</a> defeated Takeda Katsuyori near Nagashino Castle in Mikawa, killing many Takeda generals. The familiar tale of volleys fired in turn by 3,000 guns is now thought a later invention, and recent research stresses allied numbers, firepower and supplies.</em></p>

